  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improving the Quality of Basic Education in Northern Ghana</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">In Ghana primary school enrolment averages 85%, but over-stressed state infrastructure has resulted in declining literacy rates. The Northern Region is the poorest in Ghana, and has the lowest literacy rate. Learners here receive 30% less education resources per pupil than southern counterparts, and the Basic Education Certificate pass rate in many districts is in single figures and falling: for example, the pass rate in the Chereponi district fell from 9% to 6.5% between 2009 and 2011. Four key challenges hinder literacy competencies of children in lower primary: most children belong to minority language groups and start school with no English - taught in English by poorly or untrained teachers with few resources, these children struggle to learn; most learning is by rote &#x2013; activity and child-centred learning is deeper and often quicker, therefore effecting better outcomes; teachers have no on-the-job training and inadequate support - they lack motivation and absenteeism is a major problem; the position of women and girls in education is problematic, and challenging the inherent sexism implicit in teaching methods is an important to create an environment where girls can flourish. This project will introduce sustainable changes to the teaching methods used in lower primary, creating a basis for better learning for children as they progress through school. The project combines the introduction of mother-tongue, gender sensitive, child-centred teaching methods matched with appropriate resource materials, and strengthening on-going training and teacher supervision. As a result of project activities, 47,000 children will receive quality basic education; and 460 schools across 11 districts will have the capacity and support to deliver improved quality education on an on-going basis. Some 82,500 additional children will benefit in the year following the project if strongly planned and supported local uptake occurs.</narrative>
  </description>
